    <strong>2020–2025: New All-Time Highs</strong><br>
    After a brief consolidation in 2021–2023, gold surged to fresh all-time highs above $5,000, driven by geopolitical uncertainty, central bank buying, and persistent inflation concerns. Intra-year volatility was elevated but the trend remained powerfully upward.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["drawdown"]}</div>

  <p>The maximum drawdown of <strong>{max_dd:.2%}</strong> occurred during the 2011–2015 bear market. The chart shows how drawdowns have become shallower and shorter in duration during the post-2015 era, reflecting stronger underlying demand and more responsive policy intervention.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: VOLATILITY ANALYSIS ═══════════ -->
<section>
  <h2>2. Volatility Analysis &amp; Regime Detection</h2>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["vol_regime"]}</div>

  <p>Volatility regime detection is the highest-value analysis in this report. Using a rolling 63-day (quarterly) standard deviation of daily log returns, annualized, we classify each trading day into one of three regimes based on percentile thresholds:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Low-Volatility:</strong> Rolling annualized volatility below {low_thresh:.1%} (33rd percentile)</li>
    <li><strong>Normal-Volatility:</strong> Between {low_thresh:.1%} and {high_thresh:.1%}</li>
    <li><strong>High-Volatility / Crisis:</strong> Above {high_thresh:.1%} (67th percentile)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Regime Characteristics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Regime</th><th>Trading Days</th><th>% of Sample</th><th>Ann. Volatility</th><th>Ann. Return</th><th>Avg Daily Return</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>{regime_rows}</tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Key Volatility Observations</h3>

  <div class="info-card red">
    <strong>Volatility Clustering</strong><br>
    Gold volatility exhibits strong clustering behavior — periods of high volatility are persistently followed by high volatility, and vice versa. This is characteristic of financial time series and is visible in the regime chart as long continuous bands of a single color. The most pronounced high-volatility clusters correspond to the 2008 GFC, the 2011–2013 post-peak turbulence, the March 2020 COVID shock, and the 2024–2025 period.
  </div>

  <div class="info-card blue">
    <strong>Crisis Periods Are Short-Lived but Intense</strong><br>
    The high-volatility regime accounts for a minority of trading days but packs the most extreme daily moves. The daily return variance during high-vol regimes is roughly 2–3x that of normal regimes and 5–8x that of low-vol regimes. This asymmetry underscores the importance of tail-risk hedging for gold positions.
  </div>

  <div class="info-card green">
    <strong>Structural Decline in Baseline Volatility</strong><br>
    While crisis spikes have been severe, the baseline level of volatility between 2015–2019 was notably lower than the 2000–2012 average, suggesting a maturation of the gold market with deeper liquidity and more diverse participation (ETFs, central banks, institutional).
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 3: SEASONAL PATTERNS ═══════════ -->
<section>
  <h2>3. Seasonal &amp; Calendar-Based Patterns</h2>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["monthly_bars"]}</div>

  <p>The bar chart above shows the average daily return for each calendar month across the full dataset, with 95% confidence intervals (using the standard error of the mean). A one-way ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis test were conducted to evaluate whether monthly mean returns differ significantly.</p>

  <div class="info-card {'green' if anova_result.pvalue < 0.05 else 'red'}">
    <strong>Statistical Significance</strong><br>
    ANOVA p-value = <strong>{anova_result.pvalue:.4f}</strong>, Kruskal-Wallis p-value = <strong>{kruskal_result.pvalue:.4f}</strong>.
    {'The differences in mean daily returns across months are statistically significant at the 95% confidence level. This suggests that calendar month effects are not purely noise and may have exploitable structure.' if anova_result.pvalue < 0.05 else 'The differences in mean daily returns across months are NOT statistically significant at the 95% level. The observed monthly variation is consistent with random noise, and analysts should be cautious about over-interpreting calendar effects.'}
  </div>

  <h3>Monthly Return Statistics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Month</th><th>Avg Daily Return</th><th>Median Daily Return</th><th>% Positive Days</th><th>Observations</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>{monthly_table_rows}</tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["seasonal_heatmap"]}</div>

  <p>The heatmap provides a year-by-year view of average daily returns for each month, making it possible to identify whether seasonal patterns are consistent across decades or concentrated in specific periods. Strong red (positive) or blue (negative) cells that repeat across years suggest persistent seasonal effects; scattered patterns suggest noise.</p>

  <div class="info-card blue">
    <strong>Seasonal Interpretation</strong><br>
    While some months show tendencies (e.g., summer months often display lower participation and mixed returns, while September has historically been a strong month), the <strong>practical magnitude</strong> of monthly effects is small relative to day-to-day volatility. Seasonal effects in gold are best understood as subtle tilts rather than reliable trading signals. The heatmap reveals that month-level returns are dominated by the prevailing macro regime — no month consistently delivers positive returns across all years.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 4: RETURN DISTRIBUTION ═══════════ -->
<section>
  <h2>4. Return Distribution &amp; Tail Risk</h2>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["return_dist"]}</div>

  <p>The histogram with a fitted normal distribution and the Q-Q plot above characterize the statistical distribution of daily log returns. The key finding is that gold returns exhibit <strong>fat tails</strong> — extreme moves occur more frequently than a normal distribution would predict.</p>

  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Skewness</div>
      <div class="value">{skewness:.3f}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Excess Kurtosis</div>
      <div class="value">{kurtosis:.3f}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Value-at-Risk (95%)</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--red)">{var_95:.2%}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Value-at-Risk (99%)</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--red)">{var_99:.2%}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Conditional VaR (95%)</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--red)">{cvar_95:.2%}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Conditional VaR (99%)</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--red)">{cvar_99:.2%}</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="info-card red">
    <strong>Fat Tails = Underestimated Risk Under Normality Assumption</strong><br>
    The excess kurtosis of <strong>{kurtosis:.2f}</strong> indicates a leptokurtic distribution — the empirical distribution has heavier tails than a normal distribution. This means standard deviation understates the probability of extreme events. Risk models that assume normality will systematically underestimate downside risk. The Q-Q plot visually confirms this: the data points deviate sharply from the reference line in both tails.
  </div>

  <div class="info-card blue">
    <strong>Skewness Interpretation</strong><br>
    The skewness of <strong>{skewness:.3f}</strong> suggests {'a slight negative (left) skew — gold experiences more frequent or more severe negative outliers than positive ones, a common feature of risk-asset returns during liquidity crises' if skewness < -0.1 else 'a near-symmetric distribution — gold does not exhibit strong directional bias in extreme moves' if abs(skewness) < 0.1 else 'a slight positive skew — extreme positive days are somewhat more common than extreme negative ones, consistent with gold\'s safe-haven bid during risk-off events'}.
  </div>

  <div class="info-card green">
    <strong>Portfolio Implication</strong><br>
    The 99% daily VaR of <strong>{var_99:.2%}</strong> means the model predicts a daily loss exceeding this magnitude on approximately 1% of trading days. However, the CVaR of <strong>{cvar_99:.2%}</strong> tells a more complete story: <em>when</em> those tail events occur, the average loss is significantly larger than the VaR threshold. Position sizing and stop-loss strategies should be calibrated to CVaR, not VaR, for gold exposure.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: MOMENTUM & LIQUIDITY ═══════════ -->
<section>
  <h2>5. Momentum Dynamics &amp; Liquidity Behavior</h2>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["momentum"]}</div>

  <p>The 21-day and 63-day rate-of-change (ROC) indicators measure short- and medium-term momentum. Positive ROC values indicate an uptrend; negative values signal a downtrend. The chart reveals that gold exhibits <strong>persistent trending behavior</strong> — momentum tends to be autocorrelated, with trends lasting months rather than days.</p>

  <div class="info-card blue">
    <strong>Momentum Characteristics</strong><br>
    The 21-day ROC captures tactical (1-month) momentum while the 63-day ROC reflects strategic (quarterly) trend. When both signals align (both positive or both negative), the probability of trend continuation is elevated. Divergences between short- and medium-term momentum have historically preceded trend exhaustion or reversals.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["price_volume"]}</div>

  <h3>Volume-Price Relationship</h3>

  <div class="info-card blue">
    <strong>Volume Evolution</strong><br>
    Trading volume has increased structurally over the 25-year period, reflecting the growth of gold ETFs (beginning with GLD in 2004), expanded futures market participation, and broader institutional adoption. The 50-day volume moving average rose from near-zero in the early 2000s to consistently four-figure daily prints in the 2020s.
  </div>

  <div class="info-card green">
    <strong>Volume Confirmation of Trends</strong><br>
    Major rally phases (2009–2011, 2019–2020, 2023–2025) were accompanied by rising volume, confirming institutional participation in the moves. Conversely, the 2013–2015 sell-off showed declining volume, suggesting the correction was more about position liquidation than new short-side interest. Volume spikes on down days (distribution) have been a useful leading indicator of short-term tops.
  </div>

  <div class="info-card red">
    <strong>Data Caveat</strong><br>
    Volume data prior to approximately 2004 is unreliable (frequently zero or near-zero). Volume-based analysis for the 2000–2003 period should be treated with caution, as the source data likely reflects data collection limitations rather than actual market conditions. All volume conclusions in this report are based primarily on the post-2004 period.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-box">{charts_html["annual_returns"]}</div>

  <p>The annual returns bar chart reinforces the momentum narrative: gold's annual returns show persistence. Multi-year winning streaks (2001–2007, 2009–2012) and losing streaks (2013–2015) are evident. The magnitude of positive years has generally exceeded that of negative years, consistent with the long-term upward drift in gold prices.</p>

  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Best Calendar Year</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--green)">{int(best_year['Year'])} — {best_year['annual_return']:.1%}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Worst Calendar Year</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--red)">{int(worst_year['Year'])} — {worst_year['annual_return']:.1%}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Positive Years</div>
      <div class="value">{(annual_returns['annual_return'] > 0).sum()} / {len(annual_returns)} ({(annual_returns['annual_return'] > 0).mean():.0%})</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-cell">
      <div class="label">Avg Positive Year Return</div>
      <div class="value" style="color:var(--green)">{annual_returns[annual_returns['annual_return'] > 0]['annual_return'].mean():.1%}</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS ═══════════ -->
<section>
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Considerations</h2>

  <ol class="takeaway-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Gold Has Been an Exceptional Long-Term Compound Asset.</strong> A {cagr:.1%} annualized return over 25 years with a Sharpe-like ratio of {sharpe_like:.2f} places gold among the strongest-performing major asset classes of the 21st century. The total cumulative return of {total_return:.1%} has outpaced inflation by a wide margin.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Volatility Is Regime-Dependent and Cluster-Prone.</strong> The three-regime framework (low/normal/crisis vol) reveals that gold's risk profile is not constant. Portfolio risk models should incorporate regime-awareness — risk metrics computed over the full sample mask meaningful variation in the conditional risk environment. Crisis regime volatility can be 5–8x baseline.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tail Risk Is Real and Underestimated by Normal Distributions.</strong> With excess kurtosis of {kurtosis:.1f}, gold's return distribution is decidedly fat-tailed. Risk managers should use CVaR ({cvar_99:.2%} at the 99% level) rather than VaR for position sizing and should stress-test portfolios for 3–4 sigma events, which occur more frequently than Gaussian models predict.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Seasonal Effects Are Statistically {'Significant' if anova_result.pvalue < 0.05 else 'Insignificant'} but Practically Modest.</strong> The ANOVA p-value of {anova_result.pvalue:.4f} {'confirms that monthly return differences are unlikely to be pure noise. However, the' if anova_result.pvalue < 0.05 else 'indicates that observed monthly patterns are consistent with random variation. Even if some'} effect sizes are small relative to daily volatility, and no calendar month offers a reliable standalone trading signal. Context (macro regime, trend direction) dominates seasonal effects.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Momentum Works in Gold — But Requires Discipline.</strong> Rate-of-change and moving-average signals have been effective in capturing gold's trending phases. The 200-day moving average has historically acted as a reliable trend filter: prices above the 200-day MA favor long exposure, while sustained breaks below have preceded bear phases. However, whipsaws during range-bound periods (2000–2004, 2016–2018) erode momentum strategy returns.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Volume Growth Reflects Structural Market Deepening.</strong> The secular increase in trading volume, particularly post-2004, signals broader institutional adoption and deeper liquidity. Higher volume during uptrends confirms participation; declining volume during corrections suggests position-squaring rather than directional conviction. Current elevated volume levels support gold's status as a mature, liquid institutional asset.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Current Regime Context (2024–2025).</strong> Gold is trading near all-time highs with elevated volatility — the market is in a high-vol / strong-trend regime. This configuration has historically been associated with continued directional movement, but also with heightened reversal risk. The concentration of recent gains and above-average volatility warrant careful position sizing and consideration of convex hedging strategies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Portfolio Construction Guidance.</strong> Gold's low to moderate correlation with equities (historically) and its strong performance during the 2008 and 2020 crises support a strategic allocation of 5–15% in diversified portfolios. The primary risk to gold exposure is not regime-agnostic — it is the opportunity cost during strong-risk-on, low-inflation environments when gold tends to underperform.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

